- | --- | --- | --- |\n47\t| `SWARM ROUTER KEY` | `160f2dcf` | false | null | null | null |\n48\t\n49\tWindows Codex confirmed:\n50\t\n51\t- The runtime item `op://Vault/OpenRouter API Key/credential` maps to `SWARM ROUTER KEY`.\n52\t- The vault `.op/openrouter.env` fallback path maps to the same runtime item.\n53\t- The Management Key call succeeded for read-only inventory.\n54\t- No keys were created, renamed, rotated, disabled, deleted, or limit-modified.\n55\t\n56\tMac Codex confirmed:\n57\t\n58\t- Mac Hermes `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is also a runtime key, not a management key.\n59\t- `/api/v1/key` returned `is_management_key: false`.\n60\t- `/api/v1/key` returned `is_provisioning_key: false`.\n61\t- Usage metadata matched the Windows inventory values for `SWARM ROUTER KEY`.\n62\t- OpenCode stores an OpenRouter API credential in `~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json`.\n63\t- OpenCode's OpenRouter credential is also a runtime key, not a management key.\n64\t- OpenCode's `/api/v1/key` metadata matched Mac Hermes metadata for the same shared runtime key.\n65\t\n66\tTherefore, current evidence indicates Mac Hermes, Mac OpenCode, Windows runtime paths, and the vault fallback path are using the same shared OpenRouter runtime key.\n67\t\n68\tLogan added several BYOK routes on 2026-05-20. Current `/api/v1/key` metadata now shows BYOK usage and `include_byok_in_limit: true` for the runtime key. This is expected in light of the BYOK change and should not be treated as unexplained drift by itself.\n69\t\n70\t## Current Risk Picture\n71\t\n72\tThe shared runtime key is operationally simple but weak for attribution and blast-radius control.\n73\t\n74\tIf Hermes, Codex, OpenCode, and OpenClaw-adjacent tools all use `SWARM ROUTER KEY`, OpenRouter usage cannot be cleanly attributed by key metadata. A future runaway or misconfigured agent would be harder to isolate.\n75\t\n76\tThe prior Hermes `403 Budget limit exceeded (daily limit)` is **not explained by the runtime key's own key-level limit**, because:\n77\t\n78\t- `SWARM ROUTER KEY` is enabled.\n79\t- `SWARM ROUTER KEY` has no key-level limit.\n80\t- `SWARM ROUTER KEY` has no key-level remaining cap.\n81\t- BYOK daily usage was zero during the Windows inventory.\n82\t\n83\tMore likely explanations remain:\n84\t\n85\t- account-level or workspace-level budget policy\n86\t- reset-window timing\n87\t- upstream provider or BYOK policy\n88\t- model/provider-route-specific rejection\n89\t- a different runtime path at the time of the failure\n90\t- unclear OpenRouter error surfacing from a provider-layer rejection\n91\t\n92\t## Hermes Status\n93\t\n94\tHermes on Mac is currently usable again.\n95\t\n96\tRecent reliability work established:\n97\t\n98\t- Hermes can converse interactively.\n99\t- Messaging smoke tests through Discord, Telegram, and WhatsApp succeeded from Hermes.\n100\t- Hermes OpenRouter calls encountered the budget-related `403`, but fallback routing recovered after configuration/code fixes.\n101\t- Mac Hermes can use direct Mistral fallback when OpenRouter fails.\n102\t- `hermes -z` one-shot mode was patched to actually pass configured fallback chains into `AIAgent`.\n103\t- Hermes title generation was moved off the noisy OpenRouter path to avoid secondary `403` failures after successful fallback.\n104\t\n105\tCurrent practical meaning:\n106\t\n107\t- OpenRouter is still useful, but not the only route.\n108\t- Direct Mistral fallback is an important reliability leg.\n109\t- Hermes should not be treated as fully OpenRouter-reliable until OpenRouter account/workspace/provider behavior is better understood.\n110\t\n111\t## OpenClaw / Windows Link Status\n112\t\n113\tOpenClaw Mac-to-Windows pairing is stable at the transport level.\n114\t\n115\tKnown stable state from prior verification:\n116\t\n117\t- Mac OpenClaw gateway: `2026.5.16-beta.3`.\n118\t- Windows OpenClaw node: `2026.5.16-beta.3`.\n119\t- Windows node: `Windows-ZBFURY`.\n120\t- Mac gateway is loopback-bound.\n121\t- Windows reaches Mac gateway through SSH tunnel.\n122\t- Windows node is paired and connected.\n123\t- Prior stress test: `15/15` successful Mac-to-Windows invokes.\n124\t\n125\tImportant caveat:\n126\t\n127\t- Generic shell execution through `openclaw nodes invoke` remains intentionally constrained.\n128\t- This is the right security posture for management-key work.\n129\t- Do not tunnel arbitrary secret-bearing shell commands through OpenClaw unless a scoped, approved execution path exists.\n130\t\n131\tOpenClaw model-provider configuration on Mac:\n132\t\n133\t- OpenRouter plugin enabled.\n134\t- OpenRouter provider base URL: `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`.\n135\t- OpenRouter API mode: OpenAI-compatible completions.\n136\t- Primary model: `openrouter/mistralai/mistral-medium-3-5`.\n137\t- Fallbacks:\n138\t  - `openrouter/mistralai/mistral-small-2603`\n139\t  - `openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6`\n140\t  - `openrouter/openai/gpt-5.3-codex`\n141\t  - `openrouter/mistralai/mistral-large-2512`\n142\t- Secret audit clean: `plaintext=0`, `unresolved=0`, `shadowed=0`, `legacy=0`.\n143\t- OpenRouter SecretRef provider is configured and redacted by `openclaw config get`, as expected.\n144\t\n145\t## BEEFSTACK Alignment\n146\t\n147\tThe current state supports Logan's redundancy doctrine:\n148\t\n149\t```text\n150\tOllama + OpenRouter + OpenCode\n151\t```\n152\t\n153\tInterpreted as:\n154\t\n155\t- Ollama for local/simple calls where hardware and latency permit.\n156\t- OpenRouter for cloud model breadth, complex calls, and hosted fallback.\n157\t- OpenCode as a coding execution surface.\n158\t- Direct provider calls, especially Mistral direct, as a contingency when OpenRouter or local models fail.\n159\t\n160\tPreference order remains:\n161\t\n162\t1. Mistral / Claude\n163\t2. ChatGPT-Codex\n164\t3. Grok / Perplexity\n165\t4. Meta-Llama\n166\t5. Proton-Lumo\n167\t6. Moonshot-Kimi / DeepSeek\n168\t7. Microsoft-Copilot / GitHub-Copilot\n169\t\n170\tAvoid for active routing:\n171\t\n172\t- Phi\n173\t- Qwen\n174\t- Gemma\n175\t- Gemini\n176\t\n177\tGemini/Google credentials may still be appropriate for TTS or Google Cloud infrastructure when explicitly scoped.\n178\t\n179\t## Management Key Doctrine\n180\t\n181\tThe OpenRouter Management Key should be treated as privileged infrastructure control.\n182\t\n183\tGood agentic uses:\n184\t\n185\t- list runtime keys\n186\t- inspect key metadata\n187\t- produce usage reports\n188\t- detect stale/disabled/over-budget keys\n189\t- propose key separation plans\n190\t- create capped runtime keys after approval\n191\t- disable compromised keys after approval\n192\t\n193\tDo not allow:\n194\t\n195\t- arbitrary agents directly holding the Management Key\n196\t- unapproved key deletion\n197\t- unapproved key rotation\n198\t- unapproved limit increases\n199\t- silent workspace policy changes\n200\t- broad mutation without audit logs\n201\t\n202\tPreferred architecture:\n203\t\n204\t```text\n205\tagent request\n206\t-> local policy gate / broker\n207\t-> OpenRouter Management API\n208\t-> redacted result\n209\t-> durable audit log\n210\t-> Logan approval for mutations\n211\t```\n212\t\n213\t## Known Unknowns\n214\t\n215\tStill needs mapping:\n216\t\n217\t- Whether Mac OpenClaw gateway resolves to the same exact `SWARM ROUTER KEY` at call time, although model/provider config and clean SecretRef audit indicate OpenRouter is active and SecretRef-backed.\n218\t- Whether any old scripts still read `.op/openrouter.env` as plaintext fallback rather than 1Password reference.\n219\t- Whether a future dedicated Hermes runtime key should be created before changing Hermes config.\n220\t- Whether OpenRouter workspace-level budgets or guardrails explain the prior `403`.\n221\t\n222\t## Proposed Next Steps\n223\t\n224\t1. Decide whether `SWARM ROUTER KEY` should remain the emergency shared fallback.\n225\t2. If Logan approves, use the Management Key to create separate capped runtime keys:\n226\t   - `Hermes Mac Runtime Key`\n227\t   - `Windows Codex Runtime Key`\n228\t   - `OpenCode Runtime Key`\n229\t3. Update configs one surface at a time, testing after each change.\n230\t4. Record each mutation in an audit note with hash prefixes only.\n231\t5. Investigate OpenRouter workspace/account/BYOK policy if budget errors recur despite key-level limits being null.\n232\t\n233\t## Standing Rule\n234\t\n235\tUntil Logan approves otherwise:\n236\t\n237\t- read-only inventory is allowed\n238\t- redacted reporting is allowed\n239\t- key mutation is not allowed\n240\t- runtime config changes are not allowed\n241\t- full key values stay out of chat, vault, and logs\n242\t\n243\tThe world is quiet here.\n244\t"}]},"uuid":"e2cd7f43-5614-4a3e-9509-f703f1175e3c","timestamp":"2026-07-06T23:54:54.808Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/logan/IDAHO-VAULT/SNAPSHOT-OPENROUTER-HERMES-OPENCLAW-KEYS-2026-05-20.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Snapshot OpenRouter Hermes OpenClaw Keys\"\ndate: 2026-05-20\nstatus: current\nauthority: LOGAN\ndoc_class: operational_snapshot\nrelated:\n  - OpenRouter\n  - Hermes\n  - OpenClaw\n  - OpenCode\n  - BEEFSTACK\n  - Windows-ZBFURY\n  - \"!/OPENROUTER-KEYS-WINDOWS-CODEX-REPORT-2026-05-20.md\"\n  - \"!/OPENROUTER-RUNTIME-KEY-INVENTORY-WINDOWS-CODEX-2026-05-20.md\"\n  - \"!/PLAN-OPENROUTER-MANAGEMENT-KEY-WINDOWS-CODEX-2026-05-20.md\"\n  - \"!/SNAPSHOT-OPENROUTER-HERMES-BEEFSTACK-2026-05-18.md\"\n---\n\n# OpenRouter / Hermes / OpenClaw Key Snapshot - 2026-05-20\n\nThis note records the current working knowledgebase for OpenRouter key management and model-routing reliability across Mac Hermes, Mac OpenClaw, Windows Codex, and the BEEFSTACK direction.\n\nNo secret values are recorded here.\n\n## Executive Status\n\nThe OpenRouter key ambiguity is resolved.\n\nThere are two distinct 1Password items:\n\n| 1Password item | Current understanding | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `Vault/OpenRouter API Key` | `SWARM ROUTER KEY` | Runtime inference key for model calls. |\n| `Vault/OpenRouter Key` | OpenRouter Management Key | Administrative key for listing/managing runtime keys through `/api/v1/keys`. |\n\nThe `SWARM ROUTER KEY` is **not** a management key.\n\nThe Management Key is **not** for model calls.\n\n## Confirmed Runtime Key Facts\n\nWindows Codex read-only inventory found exactly one visible runtime key through the OpenRouter Management API:\n\n| label | hash prefix | disabled | key-level limit | reset | expiration |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `SWARM ROUTER KEY` | `160f2dcf` | false | null | null | null |\n\nWindows Codex confirmed:\n\n- The runtime item `op://Vault/OpenRouter API Key/credential` maps to `SWARM ROUTER KEY`.\n- The vault `.op/openrouter.env` fallback path maps to the same runtime item.\n- The Management Key call succeeded for read-only inventory.\n- No keys were created, renamed, rotated, disabled, deleted, or limit-modified.\n\nMac Codex confirmed:\n\n- Mac Hermes `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is also a runtime key, not a management key.\n- `/api/v1/key` returned `is_management_key: false`.\n- `/api/v1/key` returned `is_provisioning_key: false`.\n- Usage metadata matched the Windows inventory values for `SWARM ROUTER KEY`.\n- OpenCode stores an OpenRouter API credential in `~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json`.\n- OpenCode's OpenRouter credential is also a runtime key, not a management key.\n- OpenCode's `/api/v1/key` metadata matched Mac Hermes metadata for the same shared runtime key.\n\nTherefore, current evidence indicates Mac Hermes, Mac OpenCode, Windows runtime paths, and the vault fallback path are using the same shared OpenRouter runtime key.\n\nLogan added several BYOK routes on 2026-05-20. Current `/api/v1/key` metadata now shows BYOK usage and `include_byok_in_limit: true` for the runtime key. This is expected in light of the BYOK change and should not be treated as unexplained drift by itself.\n\n## Current Risk Picture\n\nThe shared runtime key is operationally simple but weak for attribution and blast-radius control.\n\nIf Hermes, Codex, OpenCode, and OpenClaw-adjacent tools all use `SWARM ROUTER KEY`, OpenRouter usage cannot be cleanly attributed by key metadata. A future runaway or misconfigured agent would be harder to isolate.\n\nThe prior Hermes `403 Budget limit exceeded (daily limit)` is **not explained by the runtime key's own key-level limit**, because:\n\n- `SWARM ROUTER KEY` is enabled.\n- `SWARM ROUTER KEY` has no key-level limit.\n- `SWARM ROUTER KEY` has no key-level remaining cap.\n- BYOK daily usage was zero during the Windows inventory.\n\nMore likely explanations remain:\n\n- account-level or workspace-level budget policy\n- reset-window timing\n- upstream provider or BYOK policy\n- model/provider-route-specific rejection\n- a different runtime path at the time of the failure\n- unclear OpenRouter error surfacing from a provider-layer rejection\n\n## Hermes Status\n\nHermes on Mac is currently usable again.\n\nRecent reliability work established:\n\n- Hermes can converse interactively.\n- Messaging smoke tests through Discord, Telegram, and WhatsApp succeeded from Hermes.\n- Hermes OpenRouter calls encountered the budget-related `403`, but fallback routing recovered after configuration/code fixes.\n- Mac Hermes can use direct Mistral fallback when OpenRouter fails.\n- `hermes -z` one-shot mode was patched to actually pass configured fallback chains into `AIAgent`.\n- Hermes title generation was moved off the noisy OpenRouter path to avoid secondary `403` failures after successful fallback.\n\nCurrent practical meaning:\n\n- OpenRouter is still useful, but not the only route.\n- Direct Mistral fallback is an important reliability leg.\n- Hermes should not be treated as fully OpenRouter-reliable until OpenRouter account/workspace/provider behavior is better understood.\n\n## OpenClaw / Windows Link Status\n\nOpenClaw Mac-to-Windows pairing is stable at the transport level.\n\nKnown stable state from prior verification:\n\n- Mac OpenClaw gateway: `2026.5.16-beta.3`.\n- Windows OpenClaw node: `2026.5.16-beta.3`.\n- Windows node: `Windows-ZBFURY`.\n- Mac gateway is loopback-bound.\n- Windows reaches Mac gateway through SSH tunnel.\n- Windows node is paired and connected.\n- Prior stress test: `15/15` successful Mac-to-Windows invokes.\n\nImportant caveat:\n\n- Generic shell execution through `openclaw nodes invoke` remains intentionally constrained.\n- This is the right security posture for management-key work.\n- Do not tunnel arbitrary secret-bearing shell commands through OpenClaw unless a scoped, approved execution path exists.\n\nOpenClaw model-provider configuration on Mac:\n\n- OpenRouter plugin enabled.\n- OpenRouter provider base URL: `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`.\n- OpenRouter API mode: OpenAI-compatible completions.\n- Primary model: `openrouter/mistralai/mistral-medium-3-5`.\n- Fallbacks:\n  - `openrouter/mistralai/mistral-small-2603`\n  - `openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6`\n  - `openrouter/openai/gpt-5.3-codex`\n  - `openrouter/mistralai/mistral-large-2512`\n- Secret audit clean: `plaintext=0`, `unresolved=0`, `shadowed=0`, `legacy=0`.\n- OpenRouter SecretRef provider is configured and redacted by `openclaw config get`, as expected.\n\n## BEEFSTACK Alignment\n\nThe current state supports Logan's redundancy doctrine:\n\n```text\nOllama + OpenRouter + OpenCode\n```\n\nInterpreted as:\n\n- Ollama for local/simple calls where hardware and latency permit.\n- OpenRouter for cloud model breadth, complex calls, and hosted fallback.\n- OpenCode as a coding execution surface.\n- Direct provider calls, especially Mistral direct, as a contingency when OpenRouter or local models fail.\n\nPreference order remains:\n\n1. Mistral / Claude\n2. ChatGPT-Codex\n3. Grok / Perplexity\n4. Meta-Llama\n5. Proton-Lumo\n6. Moonshot-Kimi / DeepSeek\n7. Microsoft-Copilot / GitHub-Copilot\n\nAvoid for active routing:\n\n- Phi\n- Qwen\n- Gemma\n- Gemini\n\nGemini/Google credentials may still be appropriate for TTS or Google Cloud infrastructure when explicitly scoped.\n\n## Management Key Doctrine\n\nThe OpenRouter Management Key should be treated as privileged infrastructure control.\n\nGood agentic uses:\n\n- list runtime keys\n- inspect key metadata\n- produce usage reports\n- detect stale/disabled/over-budget keys\n- propose key separation plans\n- create capped runtime keys after approval\n- disable compromised keys after approval\n\nDo not allow:\n\n- arbitrary agents directly holding the Management Key\n- unapproved key deletion\n- unapproved key rotation\n- unapproved limit increases\n- silent workspace policy changes\n- broad mutation without audit logs\n\nPreferred architecture:\n\n```text\nagent request\n-> local policy gate / broker\n-> OpenRouter Management API\n-> redacted result\n-> durable audit log\n-> Logan approval for mutations\n```\n\n## Known Unknowns\n\nStill needs mapping:\n\n- Whether Mac OpenClaw gateway resolves to the same exact `SWARM ROUTER KEY` at call time, although model/provider config and clean SecretRef audit indicate OpenRouter is active and SecretRef-backed.\n- Whether any old scripts still read `.op/openrouter.env` as plaintext fallback rather than 1Password reference.\n- Whether a future dedicated Hermes runtime key should be created before changing Hermes config.\n- Whether OpenRouter workspace-level budgets or guardrails explain the prior `403`.\n\n## Proposed Next Steps\n\n1. Decide whether `SWARM ROUTER KEY` should remain the emergency shared fallback.\n2. If Logan approves, use the Management Key to create separate capped runtime keys:\n   - `Hermes Mac Runtime Key`\n   - `Windows Codex Runtime Key`\n   - `OpenCode Runtime Key`\n3. Update configs one surface at a time, testing after each change.\n4. Record each mutation in an audit note with hash prefixes only.\n5. Investigate OpenRouter workspace/account/BYOK policy if budget errors recur despite key-level limits being null.\n\n## Standing Rule\n\nUntil Logan approves otherwise:\n\n- read-only inventory is allowed\n- redacted reporting is allowed\n- key mutation is not allowed\n- runtime config changes are not allowed\n- full key values stay out of chat, vault, and logs\n\nThe world is quiet here.\n","numLines":244,"startLine":1,"totalLines":244}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"077b8692-4b73-4dab-bfb8-72b54d5b97c8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/logan","sessionId":"4f03d270-3e64-41cc-b325-30871ab76d55","version":"2.1.202","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"your-18-entry-list-is-cached-gadget"}
